WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:06.189
The second type of error is often the toughest
to discover, a logic error.

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:10.480
With a logic error, there is no syntax problem
with the code, and the code does not cause

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.119
a crash, but the code does not perform the
way one expects it to when the code is run.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.200
We saw, in an earlier domain, how the order
of the code determines the logic in how code

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.759
flows, and sometimes, we need to rearrange
code to get it to flow in the correct order

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.109
logically.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:33.340
However, many logic errors stem from either
flawed formulas or the wrong information being

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.200
given to someone to use in a formula.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.510
Take, for example, this 512-logic file.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:45.930
I’m only focusing on one piece of logic
in this code so we can better identify logic

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:46.930
errors.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:52.300
We have three variables on lines 1 through
3, the second variable being a calculation.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.440
Then, on line 5, we have a print statement
that calculates the score plus the bonus and

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.100
then applies the multiplier, or so we think.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.270
I have comments in the code on line 2 to calculate
the bonus and then the expected result on

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.439
line 5.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:11.409
Let’s run this code.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:20.180
The result is 3600, not 6600.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:34.329
Logically, one could argue that 3000 plus
300 is 3300, and multiplied by two is 6600.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:39.400
But do you spot the logic problem in the code?

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.329
It’s not that the variable on line 2 is
derived from line 1’s value.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:46.340
That happens quite a bit in programming.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:53.189
We need to know and apply the order of operations
to identify the logic error, a concept we

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:55.220
covered in detail earlier in the course.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:02.250
What’s happening with the code is that the
bonus and multiplier calculation is happening

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:08.629
first because multiplication happens before
addition in order of operations.

00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:15.740
Thus, the logic fix is to add parentheses
around the score plus the bonus to make that

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.650
calculation happen first.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:32.500
I will make the change, rerun the code, and
get the anticipated result of 6600.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.870
Identifying logic errors is crucial to working
well with any programming language, as logic

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.140
errors do not return any error message and
thus can be very tough and time-consuming

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.140
to identify.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:49.889
The more we know what types of logic errors
to expect, the easier they are to identify.

